WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.570
Hello, and welcome to this video on Avoidance Systems.

2
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:11.929
So in this video, we're just going to look at what your different options are when it

3
00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:19.190
comes to getting AI to avoid itself via this sort of navigation.

4
00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:27.089
So to start with, this is, if you have a crowd, and we're going to see this image

5
00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:33.490
a couple of times, if they are set to walk directly into each other, you're going to see

6
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:37.409
that you get a kind of effect that works like this.

7
00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:42.409
They're going to ram into each other, and the ones on the edge will start peeling off

8
00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:45.250
after they get a chance.

9
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:52.929
But it's very common with your standard avoided system, you will get a lot of them

10
00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:55.049
stuck in different areas.

11
00:00:55.450 --> 00:01:01.409
Now, this is hard to completely avoid, and you may have to look at some code that if

12
00:01:01.409 --> 00:01:07.370
they know that they're not moving, then they start to reconsider their path.

13
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.739
But let's have a look at a couple of the options that we have built into the engine.

14
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:21.730
So the first option is called RVO, the Reciprocal Velocity Obstacle.

15
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:27.650
So this system, the actors will push each other away, other agents as they encounter

16
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.329
them, meaning that they will adapt their path to avoid each other.

17
00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:38.090
So we can see in this case, we spot that there's a sort of unnatural movement as

18
00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.590
different characters are getting pushed away from the other.

19
00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.430
Now, this is quite a cheap and simple way of doing it.

20
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.459
And in a lot of cases, it will work.

21
00:01:48.459 --> 00:01:54.719
I'll just quickly show you how to turn that on within the engine.

22
00:01:54.719 --> 00:01:56.200
So here we are.

23
00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.280
I've just opened up the top down character to demonstrate this.

24
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:06.959
Now, this is a function of the character movement component.

25
00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:12.280
So this would be the same on any AI which is using the character movement.

26
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.719
And it's a quick, simple tick box to use.

27
00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:21.840
So if I came over into the details with this selected typed in RVO, you can see that there

28
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.960
is a tick box here for use RVO avoidance.

29
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:32.860
And if I'd click that, then the character would use the avoidance.

30
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.979
Our next option is to use something called the Btorch Crowd Manager.

31
00:02:38.979 --> 00:02:46.580
Now, if you watch this, you can see this is a little bit more natural.

32
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:52.819
The characters are actually sort of changing direction, and they are taking the other actors

33
00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:54.139
into consideration.

34
00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:57.900
So it's a little bit better, a little bit more natural.

35
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:05.099
Now this actually functions as a code on the AI controller itself.

36
00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:11.479
So let me jump into the engine and show you how to turn that on.

37
00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:12.780
So here we are again.

38
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.139
We're in the top down character that we've been playing around with.

39
00:03:17.139 --> 00:03:22.280
And this time, where we want to look at it is, it's not in the character movement, this.

40
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.479
It's actually the AI controller.

41
00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:31.919
So it's going to change direction based on from the AI controller.

42
00:03:31.919 --> 00:03:39.460
So with class defaults, and usually the top selected, if we look under the details

43
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.120
panel, I'm going to search for AI.

44
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.759
And you'll see when it has Pawn in this section here.

45
00:03:45.759 --> 00:03:49.280
We have an option for the AI controller class.

46
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.599
Now if I do a drop down, I will have a few options.

47
00:03:53.599 --> 00:04:00.400
But the one that we really care about at this point is the Detour Crowd AI Controller.

48
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:07.500
So that is, just by selecting that, that's going to be a child class of the AI controller,

49
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.219
but it has some more functionality, and that's going to allow us to use that avoidance.

50
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.939
There is actually a third option, which Unreal supports, however, we're not going to go into

51
00:04:20.939 --> 00:04:29.540
that in this course, because it is advanced and would require a course in its own right.

52
00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:33.819
And that's when we're using the MassCrowd system, MassAI.

53
00:04:33.819 --> 00:04:41.540
So this is a system built for very big projects with lots and lots of different AIs, and

54
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.420
it's a different way of managing it.

55
00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:48.379
So just be aware that that exists and you can search the documentation if you want

56
00:04:48.379 --> 00:04:52.750
to know any more.

57
00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:57.829
And with that, that brings us to the end of this course.

58
00:04:57.829 --> 00:04:58.829
Thank you for watching.

59
00:04:58.829 --> 00:05:00.550
I hope that that was useful to you.
